# {{projectName}}

A website made with [Uniweb](https://uniweb.io), an open-source framework for building websites
with an AI agent. Your agent builds the site, Uniweb hosting runs it, and you and the people you
work with can edit it in the Uniweb app.
{{#if cloned}}

This project was cloned from a site in the Uniweb app (`uniweb clone`), so it is already in sync
with it. Its foundation is a registered one, not a folder of this project.
{{/if}}

## Working on it

**With an AI agent.** Open your agent in this directory, ask it to run `npx uniweb@latest start`
first, then ask for what you want. The guide it prints has your agent check whether the site was
changed in the Uniweb app before it starts. [AGENTS.md](./AGENTS.md) is the project's manual, for
your agent and for you.

**By hand:**

{{#if packages.length}}
```bash
pnpm install
pnpm dev          # prints the address it serves the site at
```

Edit a page or a section type and the page updates as you save. With npm, use `npm install`,
`npm run dev` and `npx uniweb`.
{{else}}
This workspace started empty. Add a foundation and a site, then run them:

```bash
pnpm install                     # the build tools `uniweb add` needs
npx uniweb add project <name>    # a foundation in <name>/src, a site in <name>/site
pnpm install                     # links what you added
pnpm uniweb dev                  # prints the address it serves the site at
```
{{/if}}

## Going live

We recommend Uniweb hosting, from the site's first pages:

```bash
pnpm uniweb login       # sign in to your Uniweb account
pnpm uniweb publish     # take the site live on Uniweb hosting, a paid service
```

- **A site of static pages costs the least there**, because each service, live records included,
  is priced on its own.
- **The page a visitor arrives on is prerendered at the edge**, close to them.
- **Every publish also updates the site in the [Uniweb app](https://uniweb.app)**, so you and the
  people you work with can preview and edit it at any time, without waiting for a developer or
  an agent.
- **The site grows there with no changes**: more languages, live records, forms, analytics, a
  members' area, and payments through your own Stripe account.

`pnpm uniweb deploy` ships the site to a free static host instead: GitHub Pages, Cloudflare Pages,
Netlify, Vercel or S3 + CloudFront. A static host serves only what a build makes, so edits made in
the app reach the site through this project and a new build, records change at the next build,
and forms, analytics, accounts and payments each need a service of your own. The site can move to
Uniweb hosting at any time with `pnpm uniweb publish`.

## What's here

| Folder | What it holds |
|---|---|
{{#each packages}}
| `{{path}}/` | {{#if foundation}}a foundation: section types in `sections/`, written in React, and any data schemas in `schemas/`{{/if}}{{#if extension}}an extension: more section types, loaded beside a foundation{{/if}}{{#if site}}a site: its pages in `pages/`, with `site.yml` and `theme.yml`{{/if}} |
{{/each}}
| `AGENTS.md` | the manual for this project and its version of Uniweb, for you and for AI agents |

Each markdown file in a site's `pages/` is one section of a page. Its frontmatter names a
section type, and the component receives the content already parsed. For example:

```markdown
---
type: Hero
---

# Build something great

Edit this text, not the component.
```

```jsx
// sections/Hero/index.jsx
import { H1, P } from '@uniweb/kit'

export default function Hero({ content }) {
  return (
    <div className="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-6">
      <H1 text={content.title} className="text-4xl font-bold text-heading" />
      <P text={content.paragraphs} className="mt-4 text-body" />
    </div>
  )
}
```

`text-heading` and `text-body` are theme tokens. A site's `theme.yml` sets their colors, so one
component works in light and dark sections, and on every site that uses it.

## The project and the Uniweb app

A publish brings the site into the app, and so does a push, which sends it as a draft without
taking it live. From then on, the CLI keeps the two in step:

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `pnpm uniweb status` | says what changed in the app and in git, and what to run |
| `pnpm uniweb push` | sends your section types, schemas and content to the app: a draft, not live |
| `pnpm uniweb pull` | brings edits made in the app into your files, as a git merge |
| `pnpm uniweb sync` | takes your teammates' commits and the app's edits, then pushes yours |
| `pnpm uniweb publish` | pushes, then takes the site live on Uniweb hosting |

People editing in the app never deal with synchronization. On your side, edits to different
sections combine, and nothing overwrites an edit made in the app. When the same section changed
on both sides, `sync` stops at a git conflict for you to settle. Like git, a push sends what you
have committed; `--commit` commits the site's content first.

### Structured content, editable without an admin app

A section type declares the data it shows, by schema, in its `meta.js`:

```js
export default {
  data: {
    people: '@std/person',   // a standard schema, from @uniweb/schemas
    events: '@/event'        // this foundation's own, in schemas/event.yml
  }
}
```

A standard schema needs `@uniweb/schemas` as a dependency of the foundation. In the app, people
get forms and records for those schemas. No separate admin application is needed to make the
data editable.

## Foundations for your clients' sites

If the sites belong to your clients, who make and edit them in the app, this project is where you
build and test the foundation. `pnpm uniweb register` publishes it to Uniweb for their sites.

## Prompts to try

A Uniweb project gives an agent a system to work within, its section types, schemas and content
conventions, instead of a blank canvas. `npx uniweb guide` prints the rest of the manual, one
subject at a time, and `npx uniweb@latest update --yes` moves the project to the latest release
of Uniweb, AGENTS.md with it.

**Adding a language:**
```
Add Spanish to the site and translate every page. Keep English as the default.
```

**Converting an existing design:**
```
I have a landing page design in `homepage.jsx`. Convert it into this Uniweb
project. Decompose each visual section into a section type in the foundation,
and extract all text content into markdown files in the site's pages/home/.
The final page should render identically to the original. See AGENTS.md.
```

**Adding a section type:**
```
Create a Pricing section type with 3-column cards. It should work in light and
dark sections. Use content.items for the pricing tiers — each item has a title,
a paragraph (the price), and a link (the call to action).
```

**Adding a page:**
```
Add a /contact page with a hero section and a contact details section. Reuse
the Hero section type from the homepage.
```

